00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.120
And that's really,
when we talk about API,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.640
that's the core aspect
that we're talking

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.560
about from that perspective.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.650
Those APIs are built
by an API team.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.890
Now, now we're getting a
little bit into the developers

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.750
potentially in
your organization.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.400
If you have an
API program, these

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.330
are the people building the
APIs for those developers

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.490
to build the apps
for the end consumer.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:45.930
So you're starting to see
what we mean here when

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.660
we say the digital value chain.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:49.410
They're all kind of
connected and all kind

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.670
of related in that sense.

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:54.030
And then, of course, you
know APIs themselves,

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.070
they've got to get the
data from somewhere.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:00.660
And so that's where we
consider this the back end.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:04.440
So this could be things like
databases or backend services,

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.310
things that you may want to
expose out to the developers,

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:10.470
or having that data,
that capability

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:15.060
exposed out to your developer,
but packaged up in an API.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.290
So just to reiterate,
customer, end user.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:21.330
End user uses the application.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.040
Like in this case,
from my example,

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:24.720
like a mobile application.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.780
Those applications are
built by the developer.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.510
Those developers are using APIs.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:34.440
In this case, this is when it
starts to come into your realm.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.200
Start using your APIs to
power the applications

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.490
that they're building.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:41.220
Those APIs are built
by an API team,

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.980
and then those APIs
and the API team

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:47.940
really are building APIs around
some sort of backend system

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.862
or backend service that you
may have in place already.

00:05:50.862 --> 00:05:52.320
So the topic of
this webcast, we're

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.240
really going to be
focusing on what we

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.302
call the consumer of the API.

00:05:57.302 --> 00:05:59.010
So you may have guessed
that already when

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:00.385
we're talking
about the consumer,

00:06:00.385 --> 00:06:03.030
we're really talking
about the developer level

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.160
that you see here.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.670
Now, when we talk
about API consumer,

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:10.830
you can break that up into
different subcategories.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:13.590
In the context of
developers, they

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.900
could be external
developers, so this

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.480
could be a person working
out of their garage

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.300
and they're building some
revolutionary new application.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.550
They have nothing really to
do with your organization.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:25.967
They're not even
really considered

00:06:25.967 --> 00:06:27.550
a partner of your organization.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.190
But they may actually
use your APIs,

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:31.170
if your APIs are
open to the public,

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:32.850
to build some sort
of functionality

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.530
for some application
that they want

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:36.660
to roll out to the end user.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:38.490
These can also be
partner developers,

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:41.070
so people that are external
to your organization,

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:44.010
but maybe like a
partner company that you

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:47.130
may work with to build
some sort of application,

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.410
a joint application
that you guys

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:52.990
want to roll out to provide
some value to your customers.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:55.050
They can also even be
internal developers.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.090
They're not really
listed here on the sheet.

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:59.520
But even internally, the
way that you can also

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.745
look at it as well is that
you may have an API programmer

00:07:02.745 --> 00:07:04.740
or a team that builds APIs.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:07.680
But your consumer is another
part of your organization,

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.780
building some sort of
internal application

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.350
to roll out to, whether
internally or even externally,

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.610
to your end consumers as well.

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:20.055
So as you can see here, when
we talk about the API consumer,

00:07:20.055 --> 00:07:23.940
we really are talking about
the consumers of the API,

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:27.720
but those consumers can be
different people, both within

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:30.220
and outside of
your organization.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:34.440
So when we talk about this whole
concept of a consumer approach,

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:38.595
these are kind of things where
you might want to ask yourself,

00:07:38.595 --> 00:07:41.070
if you have an API program
established already,

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:45.660
have you heard of any
of this feedback before?

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:48.900
Things like the
API documentation

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.410
isn't documented very
well, or I'm having trouble

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:56.180
finding the APIs that are
pertaining to the application I

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:56.910
want to build.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.790
Or the API just doesn't
do what I want it to.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:04.170
Or I just cannot figure
out how an API works.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:06.420
When we talk about a
user-centric approach

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:08.130
to API design and
API innovation,

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.320
this is really what
we want to focus on.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.750
We want to start
addressing the fact

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:16.920
that what we see as API
programs that are successful

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:20.550
really do take a
consumer-centric or

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:24.540
user-centric approach
to how they design,

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.570
how they implement, how
they roll out their APIs.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.930
So if you're hearing a lot
of these kind of feedback

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:35.250
within your own API
program, then you're

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:38.610
the target audience of who we
want to talk to, specifically

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:41.380
in the topics that we're
going to be covering later on.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.980
So one of the reasons,
or how we explain

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:47.040
why you may be getting
the feedback that we just

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.110
talked about, is
this whole concept

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:52.470
of the inside-out and
outside-in approach to APIs.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.410
So once again, I'm going to
revisit the digital value

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:56.410
chain in this case.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:58.470
So when we talk
about inside-out,

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:01.020
this is when we start talking
about-- think of it in terms

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:02.850
of how you design an API.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:07.110
Traditionally, if you look
at what I call the old school

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:10.770
model of API design, when you
start talking about API design

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:13.410
from a legacy perspective,
a lot of times what we see

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.530
is that a lot of our
customers start off

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.310
with an inside-out approach.

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:20.250
And so what I mean
by that m they

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:22.320
have some backend
service or some data

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.820
they think is
going to be useful.

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.760
And really, when they
start building APIs,

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:31.860
they're not really taking the
customer or the app developer,

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:35.460
in this case, the external
consumer, into account.

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:37.650
They're really just saying,
I have all this data.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:40.810
I have all this goodness in
terms of backend services.

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:42.940
How do I just wrap
that into an API

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:45.840
and then just sort of roll
that out there for the masses

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.030
to consume?

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:50.550
It's this whole like, "build
it and they will come"

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.370
kind of approach to API design.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:55.620
And what we see
happen is, they really

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:59.400
just kind of repackage
what the backend service

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:02.400
provides and just kind
of throw it out there.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.100
Now, the problem with
this is that a lot

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:09.280
of times, the backend
services are complex.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.280
They're not necessarily
straightforward or

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:16.080
self-explanatory when it comes
to understanding what it does,

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:17.740
even from a data perspective.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:21.090
And so if I'm a person
within the organization,

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:22.770
it may make perfect sense.

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:24.570
But if I'm that
guy who's building

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:26.970
the application
in my garage and I

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:29.520
have no idea how your
backend services work,

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:31.230
you can see a
scenario here where

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.960
that becomes a little
bit of an issue, right?

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:35.340
If I'm just getting
an API that's

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:38.730
sort of like a data dump of
all these different fields

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:44.040
and data, I may have a hard time
understanding what exactly I

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:47.280
need to extract to get my
needs as an app developer out

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:48.550
of that.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:51.570
And so that's this whole
inside-out concept.

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:54.390
Now, what we promote is sort
of the opposite of that.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:56.168
This is the outside-in approach.

00:10:56.168 --> 00:10:57.960
You guys are familiar
with this whole thing

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:02.490
of API-first method
of API design,

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:04.500
then this is sort
of related to that.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.000
And really, what
we mean by that is,

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.020
when you're designing your APIs
or you're approaching the API

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:13.290
program, you kind of want to
think of it from the other way

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:15.300
around, the opposite
of inside-out.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.250
Which would be
outside-in, obviously.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.290
And what we mean by
that is, you really

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:21.540
want to start thinking
and putting yourself

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:26.130
in the shoes of the end user
and the application developer.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:28.080
So a simple way of
thinking about it

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:31.800
was, put yourself in the shoes
of the application developer.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:35.490
If I was the person building
the app, what sort of APIs

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:38.805
would I want to consume?

00:11:38.805 --> 00:11:44.550
If I were to want the most
easy, self-explanatory API

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.980
to consume, what
would that look like?

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:50.160
And really taking that
mindset of saying what

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:52.020
are the needs of the end user?

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:54.690
What kind of applications and
data are they looking for?

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:57.420
And also, what do the
application developers

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:01.260
want in terms of how
to consume those APIs?

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:04.650
And that may or may not
correlate to the back end.

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:07.620
So I think this is where it
gets a little bit tricky.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:09.750
Because a lot of
times, especially

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:12.092
if you're internal
within your organization

00:12:12.092 --> 00:12:13.800
and you have a good
understanding of what

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:15.840
the backend services
provide, you

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:19.920
tend to want to go
for building out APIs

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.145
that you know the backend
service can accommodate.

00:12:23.145 --> 00:12:25.270
Once again, I
mentioned API-first.

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:27.270
Where it becomes a little
bit difficult for some

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:29.103
of these teams and some
of these programmers

00:12:29.103 --> 00:12:33.010
is that they have a hard time
decoupling from that model.

00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:35.280
And so they may
approach outside-in

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:38.400
with best intentions, but they
always get into a position

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:42.060
where it's really just a
slightly better version

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:43.800
of the inside-out model.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.300
What we want to
promote is really

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:49.170
just starting from a
blank slate and using

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:50.760
that as the starting point.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.370
To some extent, just forgetting
all about your backend service

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:57.030
and saying, purely from an
application perspective,

00:12:57.030 --> 00:12:58.530
what are some cool apps?

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:00.030
What are some cool
functionalities

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:01.750
that we think will be needed?

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:03.510
And then using that
as the jumping-off

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:05.950
point for your API design.

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:08.690
So once again, to emphasize
that I was talking about before,

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:11.520
your API consumers
are important.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.650
And some of the things
to consider here

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:16.050
is, you really want to know
who your app developers are

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:18.210
and the applications
that they're building.

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:21.060
I already talked the inside-out
or an outside-in to death.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:24.440
But once again, correlating with
this whole API-first mentality,

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:27.070
the API-first approach
to API design.

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:28.117
Easy to consume APIs--

00:13:28.117 --> 00:13:29.700
I'm going to mention
this a little bit

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:31.440
later in the webcast,
but things about

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:33.480
like really taking a
hard look at your APIs.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:35.880
And are they really
easy to understand,

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:38.550
if I were to just kind of go
in and look at those APIs?

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:40.650
Is it self-explanatory enough?

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:43.810
Do the field names make
sense on their own?

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:49.320
Do I need to read some sort of
long API description document

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:53.310
just to understand what
payload I'm receiving when I'm

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.440
making a call and vice versa?

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.210
You want to make the
onboarding process

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:58.320
simple and frictionless.

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:00.528
This is something, when I
talk about developer portal

00:14:00.528 --> 00:14:03.160
later on, I'll cover
in greater detail.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:05.610
But you want to make sure that
getting access to your APIs

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:09.190
is going to be a very smooth and
not a very convoluted process.

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:14.250
And then finally, build your
requirements to your consumers,

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:16.450
not based off of your
own requirements.

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:18.750
Really, this is once
again putting yourself

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:22.020
in the shoes of the app
developer and the end user

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:24.270
and building requirements
from that perspective,

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:29.250
rather than just, these
are what services we have.

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:31.560
What are the requirements
of just packaging it

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:32.805
up and getting it out there?

00:14:32.805 --> 00:14:35.430
So we're going to touch a little
bit upon developer engagement.

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:38.640
This is all sort of
related to this ongoing,

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:42.600
stressing the importance of
the API consumer approach.

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:44.400
So of course, the
developer engagement

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:47.672
is key to the success
of your API program.

00:14:47.672 --> 00:14:48.630
What does that require?

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:51.450
So these are the
three main pillars

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:53.070
that we see often
in the industry,

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:55.080
and this is something
that we promote heavily

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:56.440
with our customers.

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:59.087
First of all is this
concept of API products.

00:14:59.087 --> 00:15:01.170
Now, I'm not going to go
into great detail of what

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:02.490
an API product is.

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:06.050
There's a lot of
documentation and videos,

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:09.380
and it's one of the core
foundations of Apigee

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:13.010
as a product, is the ability
of bundling up your APIs

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:13.700
into products.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:17.540
But very high-level, 10,000-foot
description of what API

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:20.840
products are is that they're
essentially a bundle of APIs.

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:24.710
One way you can look at it, I
like to use the LEGO analogy.

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:27.350
If you guys are
building out LEGOs,

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:30.770
very rarely do you go and
buy LEGO pieces one by one

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:32.240
when you're creating something.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:35.480
More often than not, you go
into a store and you buy a set,

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:37.400
and that set comes
with all the pieces

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:40.760
you need to build
whatever plane or boat

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:42.450
or whatever you want to build.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:45.320
So you can think of API
products as that set.

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:47.180
It is a bundle of APIs.

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:49.770
In this case, it will be
the individual LEGO pieces.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:52.580
But at the end of the
day, it's a bundle of APIs

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:56.060
to have some sort of
core functionality

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:58.580
that you are providing
to your application

00:15:58.580 --> 00:16:00.470
developers and your consumers.

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:05.630
So having a great API product
methodology and rollout model

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:07.040
is key here.

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:08.720
The other bit is a
developer portal,

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:11.480
having some sort of
mechanism of communicating

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:15.650
out APIs, allowing the
developers to have a place

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:19.910
to go and register to
get access to your API,

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:23.070
as well as getting
documentation and all

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:25.280
those kind of cool
things to raise

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:28.010
some sort of an easy-to-use,
self-explanatory portal,

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:30.830
is another trend we see
as being very important.

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:34.520
And then also, when it comes
to developer enablement,

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:36.710
providing them
with the resources

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:38.480
that they need and
the support that

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:42.740
they need to be successful
in utilizing your APIs.

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:45.650
So when it comes to best
practices to promote adoption,

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:47.300
we have these four
main categories.

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:48.800
I'm going to deep
dive into each one

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:50.150
of these in just a little bit.

00:16:50.150 --> 00:16:54.350
But those are acquisition,
documentation and onboarding,

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:58.190
self-service, and of course, the
support and ongoing maintenance

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:00.360
of your API consumers.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:01.850
So when we talk
about acquisition,

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:03.800
these are the things
that we talk about.

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:07.650
This is, how do we get your
app developers onboarded

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:09.980
and how do we get
them using your APIs

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:12.140
and getting excited
to use your APIs?

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:14.790
So the first one is this concept
you may have heard about.

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:16.609
It's called the 5-5-5 Rule.

00:17:16.609 --> 00:17:19.339
Long story short, within
the first 5 seconds,

00:17:19.339 --> 00:17:21.619
you want to grab
their attention.

00:17:21.619 --> 00:17:24.440
In the next 5 seconds, you
want to build interest.

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:28.130
And then in the next
5 minutes, empowering

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:32.870
them to build an actual
case of using your APIs.

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:36.080
So once again, this
is really emphasizing

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:38.210
getting them excited
about your APIs

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:42.037
and getting them able to quickly
understand your API program.

00:17:42.037 --> 00:17:43.370
What is it trying to accomplish?

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:45.710
And really building out
a use case for those

00:17:45.710 --> 00:17:47.930
APIs in a very, very
short amount of time.

00:17:47.930 --> 00:17:49.910
That relates to some
of the other bullet

00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:53.480
points you see here, such
as value proposition.

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:55.430
How do you showcase your APIs?

00:17:55.430 --> 00:17:58.710
How do you go out and promote
these APIs and say, hey,

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:01.880
this is something you guys want
to use when you're building out

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:03.230
your applications.

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:06.110
Once again, the API market
is very, very competitive.

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:08.390
If you really think
about it, there

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:11.170
are a lot of different
APIs out there.

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:12.920
Potentially, you may
have competitors that

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:14.390
have their own API programs.

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:17.630
And if you're
really trying to win

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:20.780
over an external
application developer,

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:25.460
for example, having
the most simple-to-use,

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:28.970
robust API catalog
is really one where

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:31.130
you're going to win them over.

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:33.440
Once again, I keep going
back to this example

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:37.880
of the app developer that's
working out of the garage.

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:40.880
If they're building out
the next revolutionary app

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:42.740
and they're using
your APIs but they

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:45.350
have a lot of different
choices amongst you

00:18:45.350 --> 00:18:47.060
and your competitors
of which APIs

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:51.260
they want to utilize to get the
data that they need, of course

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:54.410
they're going to be leaning
more towards the APIs

00:18:54.410 --> 00:18:56.618
that are easier
to consume rather

00:18:56.618 --> 00:18:58.035
the ones that are
more complicated

00:18:58.035 --> 00:19:00.170
and a bit more confusing.

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:03.410
They're all about rolling
applications out very quickly,

00:19:03.410 --> 00:19:08.060
and you really want to appeal
to that aspect of it, which

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:09.740
goes into that
fourth bullet point

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:11.990
here about making it
easy to get started,

00:19:11.990 --> 00:19:13.820
making it frictionless.

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:17.120
And then the last one
is really just something

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.150
that we've seen a lot as
well, promoting hackathons.

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:23.390
This is something that we,
as Google, we do a lot,

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:24.800
as you may have heard.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:27.470
But really just having
different mechanisms

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:29.780
of really getting people to
have hands-on experiences

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:32.645
with the APIs in
order to acquire them

00:19:32.645 --> 00:19:34.910
as consumers for those APIs.

00:19:34.910 --> 00:19:37.670
These are the key tenets
that we talk about.

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:40.185
The next one is
documentation and onboarding.

00:19:40.185 --> 00:19:42.560
We're getting a little bit
into the developer portal now.

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:44.940
But really, what we're
talking about here--

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:49.040
first off, how do we engage
developers before registration?

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:52.130
This touches a little
bit upon the concept of,

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:54.890
a lot of times when it
gets access to APIs,

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:57.480
they're locked behind
this whole registration

00:19:57.480 --> 00:20:02.150
wall where if I want to
even look into the APIs that

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:05.380
are available within a
person's organization,

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:07.300
I need to go through
this whole 5-

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:10.330
to 10-step process of
getting someone registered,

00:20:10.330 --> 00:20:14.200
getting an API key, just to get
access to play around with it.

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:18.580
And we want to, however it makes
sense given your business case,

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:19.930
eliminate that model.

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:22.870
You want to start promoting
these APIs out there

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:25.930
to the public so that they
can get a good understanding

00:20:25.930 --> 00:20:27.940
of the value or the
services that you're

00:20:27.940 --> 00:20:29.890
providing without
jumping through hoops

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:31.510
to getting access.

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:34.840
Doesn't mean necessarily
that you're opening up access

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:37.510
to all your APIs to just
anyone in the public.

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:40.480
Once again, this really does
depend on your business case

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:42.700
and what services
you're providing.

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:46.420
But the more you can get
into this model of providing

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:49.600
open documentation or
descriptions of what services

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:51.670
you can provide, the
easier it's going to be

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:55.060
and the faster it's going to
be for those app developers

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:57.520
to really want to start
onboarding with your API

00:20:57.520 --> 00:20:58.570
program.

00:20:58.570 --> 00:20:59.950
When they do
eventually register,

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:02.140
you want to make sure that
registration process is

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:03.260
frictionless as well.

00:21:03.260 --> 00:21:05.860
So this really goes
into the whole concept,

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:09.250
once again, of thinking
of, when it comes--

00:21:09.250 --> 00:21:13.330
we were talking about API design
in the concept of API-first

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:17.200
or from the perspective of
an application developer.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:19.360
This is the same thing,
just from a registration

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:20.710
point of view.

00:21:20.710 --> 00:21:23.350
Your registration process
should be thought of of,

00:21:23.350 --> 00:21:26.297
if I was an application
developer, what

00:21:26.297 --> 00:21:28.630
sort of registration process
would I want to go through?

00:21:28.630 --> 00:21:33.160
I guarantee you that everyone
wants a simpler registration

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:36.040
process rather than a
very complicated one that

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:39.380
takes a week to get an API key.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:41.880
They want something quick,
they want something fast,

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:44.380
and they want something that's
very straightforward and easy

00:21:44.380 --> 00:21:46.330
to take onboard with.

00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:49.390
Self-service-- so once again, I
touched a little bit upon this

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:52.840
when I was describing
about API design early on.

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:56.030
But you want to make sure your
APIs are easy to understand.

00:21:56.030 --> 00:21:58.750
We talked a little bit about
API products here as well.

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:01.990
You want to make
sure that, when it

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:05.350
comes to creating an application
or having them playing around

00:22:05.350 --> 00:22:09.250
with your APIs, you want it to
be as self-explanatory as you

00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:10.420
can possibly be.

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:14.940
And that can be looked at from
a variety of different ways.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:18.880
You can look at it from how
these API request and response

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:21.400
payloads look like,
how simple are they

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:24.340
or how big the payloads are.

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:26.590
How granular the APIs
are, for example.

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:28.100
That's another way of doing it.

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:29.710
But really, it's
the concept of like,

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:32.030
if I'm building my
initial application

00:22:32.030 --> 00:22:33.730
and I just wanted to
quickly play around

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:36.010
with your APIs, what's
the quickest, most

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:37.180
effective way of doing that?

00:22:37.180 --> 00:22:39.983
And self-service,
self-explanatory kind of APIs

00:22:39.983 --> 00:22:42.400
is definitely the model we
recommend as definitely the way

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:43.370
to go.

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:45.880
And the API programs
that we see our customers

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:47.980
have the most
success, those APIs

00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:49.540
are very easy to
understand, just

00:22:49.540 --> 00:22:52.210
reading a one- to
two-sentence description,

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:55.210
or even to some extent,
the API name itself.

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:58.420
Just reading-- if they can read
it and get a good understanding

00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:02.770
of, oh, I know what this API
does, then the easier the

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:03.683
better.

00:23:03.683 --> 00:23:05.350
And then there's also
this whole concept

00:23:05.350 --> 00:23:06.970
of having them try this out.

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:10.480
This ties once again with the
whole registration process,

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:13.300
but also within the
capability of Apigee

00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:16.390
as a product of being
able to call APIs

00:23:16.390 --> 00:23:20.380
or doing test calls through
the portal itself is something

00:23:20.380 --> 00:23:22.128
that we see a lot
of success with.

00:23:22.128 --> 00:23:23.920
So if you can implement
something like that

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:27.370
and provide them with
some sort of "try it now"

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:29.770
feature within your
developer portal,

00:23:29.770 --> 00:23:33.640
that is the ideal
method that we recommend

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:36.520
when it comes to the ease
of onboarding and the time

00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:39.860
to get folks onboarded
onto your API program.

00:23:39.860 --> 00:23:41.920
And last but not least,
support and maintenance.

00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:46.090
This is the ongoing support,
once they are building

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:48.460
applications using your APIs.

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:53.830
What resources do they have in
case they run into roadblocks?

00:23:53.830 --> 00:23:56.260
What kind of documentation?

00:23:56.260 --> 00:23:58.780
What kind of guides do
they need to follow?

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:00.340
What sort of write-up?

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:03.910
Community-- we talk about this
whole concept of you building

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:06.880
out an API community where
you may have something

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:10.480
like a forum or a blog
or some sort of mechanism

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:13.840
where they can go in and
ask questions about like,

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:16.960
hey, I'm running into this
issue with consuming an API.

00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:18.130
How do I get around it?

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:20.650
Or I'm getting a weird response.

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:23.920
Can someone please explain
to me what this response is?

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:27.160
And really, once again,
it's providing them

00:24:27.160 --> 00:24:28.540
with the resources
that they need

00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:32.680
to be successful in using
and consuming your API.

00:24:32.680 --> 00:24:35.830
And this could be within your
developer portal, as well.

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:39.400
It could be part of a
maybe dedicated role

00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:41.680
that you have, like an API
evangelist or some sort

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:42.570
of product owner.

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:44.320
But the other way of
looking at this, too,

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:46.390
is really just
having a mechanism

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:48.130
of capturing feedback.

00:24:48.130 --> 00:24:50.230
Once your APIs
are rolled out, it

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:53.380
doesn't mean those APIs
will forever not be changed.

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:55.540
We have versioning we
want you to build out

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:57.970
your APIs in iterations
and constantly get

00:24:57.970 --> 00:25:02.540
feedback from your API
consumers and app developers

00:25:02.540 --> 00:25:06.470
so that you can further iterate
and enhance those APIs to cater

00:25:06.470 --> 00:25:09.050
to the needs of where
your business is going

00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:12.200
and, specifically, who's
consuming those APIs.

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:15.230
So here are some examples that
we've seen in the industry.

00:25:15.230 --> 00:25:16.850
I personally have
seen a lot of this

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:19.910
with a lot of our
customers over the years,

00:25:19.910 --> 00:25:22.310
when they're
talking about taking

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:25.530
this whole outside-in
approach, ease of use,

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:26.600
ease of onboarding.

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:28.160
These are some common trends.

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:29.690
I'm not going to
go into the details

00:25:29.690 --> 00:25:31.520
of each one of these
mean, because that's

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:34.310
going to be different
webcasts and different topics

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:35.810
all on its own.

00:25:35.810 --> 00:25:38.090
But I do recommend that,
if any one of these

00:25:38.090 --> 00:25:40.850
are of interest to
you, definitely hit up

00:25:40.850 --> 00:25:46.520
either our Apigee community
or our Apigee Docs page.

00:25:46.520 --> 00:25:49.220
We have a lot of good
documentation on our website

00:25:49.220 --> 00:25:52.140
that really deeps dive into a
lot of these different things,

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:54.680
whether they be industry
standards and industry

00:25:54.680 --> 00:25:58.400
trends or very specific
product capabilities.

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:01.160
That can help you out with this.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:04.790
So one of the trends that we
see is moving to RESTful APIs.

00:26:04.790 --> 00:26:06.680
A lot of our
customers, specifically

00:26:06.680 --> 00:26:09.800
those with API programs that
have been around for a while,

00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:15.260
they usually come from kind
of a SOAP-based API model,

00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:19.250
those with things like
XML payloads, for example.

00:26:19.250 --> 00:26:21.410
And having that move
over to something

00:26:21.410 --> 00:26:24.050
more RESTful,
something using JSON,

00:26:24.050 --> 00:26:26.310
from a payload perspective.

00:26:26.310 --> 00:26:29.540
That's one of the common
first steps we see,

00:26:29.540 --> 00:26:32.660
where just making the
payload easier to read,

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:36.350
easier to use from a
consumer perspective.

00:26:36.350 --> 00:26:38.510
With that, as well,
what we see a lot when

00:26:38.510 --> 00:26:41.150
it comes to RESTful
APIs, and specifically

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:43.400
in the concept of
payloads, is that a lot

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:45.920
of times, when we
see with legacy APIs,

00:26:45.920 --> 00:26:47.730
that sometimes these
payloads are huge.

00:26:47.730 --> 00:26:50.510
They're giving them
basically all the data

00:26:50.510 --> 00:26:52.880
within a specific
call, and just to comb

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:56.630
through all of that to get maybe
a specific field that the app

00:26:56.630 --> 00:27:00.470
developer needs is
a tedious thing.

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:04.130
Using something like
the Apigee API Platform

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:09.410
to customize or to
adjust the payload based

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:13.790
on maybe some sort of entry
criteria or some sort of data

00:27:13.790 --> 00:27:16.850
that they're passing as
part of the API request,

00:27:16.850 --> 00:27:19.250
is things that they can
use to really either maybe

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:24.268
shorten or filter out certain
fields pertaining to what

00:27:24.268 --> 00:27:25.560
the app developer wants to use.

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:28.060
So there's a lot of different
ways that you can approach it.

00:27:28.060 --> 00:27:30.710
But just keep in mind that
once again, this whole concept

00:27:30.710 --> 00:27:33.560
of, you want your APIs that
are self-explanatory but also

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:37.040
easy to consume, easy to
read, and easy to understand.

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:39.650
I talked about incorporating
methods of feedback.

00:27:39.650 --> 00:27:43.190
Once again, blogs, forums,
having a dedicated role

00:27:43.190 --> 00:27:47.180
to go in and ping your
application developers

00:27:47.180 --> 00:27:48.530
and saying, hey, what's working?

00:27:48.530 --> 00:27:49.520
What's not?

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:54.290
That is another way of really
allowing you to better steer

00:27:54.290 --> 00:27:57.980
your API program in the
direction of consumability

00:27:57.980 --> 00:28:02.090
and having this whole consumer
kind of approach to APIs,

00:28:02.090 --> 00:28:04.040
so that when you're
investing your time

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:07.370
and resources of building
that next version of the API,

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:09.140
you don't want to just
shoot in the dark.

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:11.630
You really want to base
it off some hard data,

00:28:11.630 --> 00:28:14.087
some hard feedback, both
positive and negative,

00:28:14.087 --> 00:28:15.920
that you're getting
from the people actually

00:28:15.920 --> 00:28:17.690
using your APIs.

00:28:17.690 --> 00:28:20.480
We talked about
developer portal already.

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:24.050
But once again, utilizing it
in a very effective manner when

00:28:24.050 --> 00:28:26.870
it comes to the
registration process, when

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:29.570
it comes to documentation, when
it comes to this whole "try it

00:28:29.570 --> 00:28:30.740
now" feature with APIs.

00:28:30.740 --> 00:28:31.800
Things like that.

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:33.050
This one's an interesting one.

00:28:33.050 --> 00:28:35.330
The whole role of
an API evangelist--

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:39.230
a lot of times, we don't
see this role when--

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:41.060
especially with our
newer customers.

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:45.590
This is sort of a new role
that only a select handful

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:50.270
of our customers have
pre-established before really

00:28:50.270 --> 00:28:52.250
rethinking their API program.

00:28:52.250 --> 00:28:54.080
And what we mean
about API evangelists

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:56.630
is sort of like the person
who promotes the API.

00:28:56.630 --> 00:28:59.150
So this could be someone
who goes out and maybe hosts

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:03.320
the hackathons or really
reaches out to your application

00:29:03.320 --> 00:29:06.200
community and saying, hey, look,
these are all the cool new APIs

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:07.242
that are being developed.

00:29:07.242 --> 00:29:09.500
This is why you want
to use these APIs.

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:13.220
And really, someone
to promote those APIs

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:14.870
is the purpose of this role.

00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:16.617
And a lot of times,
this role is also

00:29:16.617 --> 00:29:18.950
combined with the person who's
the direct person getting

00:29:18.950 --> 00:29:19.610
the feedback.

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:22.730
When we talk about the feedback,
the second bullet point here,

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:25.760
that's also kind of related
to that role as well.

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:28.250
Simplifying the API
registration process.

00:29:28.250 --> 00:29:29.967
Once again, we talked
about that already.

00:29:29.967 --> 00:29:31.800
But you want it to be
as simple as possible.

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:35.180
You don't want it to be a
one-week, two-week process

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:37.010
of running through
manual approvals

00:29:37.010 --> 00:29:39.793
and sending emails
just to get an API key.

00:29:39.793 --> 00:29:41.210
If you can make
this self-service,

00:29:41.210 --> 00:29:43.310
do a thing like a
developer portal.

00:29:43.310 --> 00:29:46.820
Make it within seconds
to get an API key

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:51.810
rather than a very manual
process that takes time.

00:29:51.810 --> 00:29:54.642
The faster, the better,
and the easier the better.

00:29:54.642 --> 00:29:56.600
And then once again, this
move to API products.

00:29:56.600 --> 00:30:01.520
Once again, it just makes
understanding the APIs easier

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:04.190
if you were to take a whole
product or a productization

00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:06.020
or a product-centric
approach to it.

00:30:06.020 --> 00:30:08.810
Because once again, going
back to the LEGO analogy,

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:11.000
it's a little bit
easier to understand

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:14.600
when it's bundled in the context
of a specific functionality

00:30:14.600 --> 00:30:18.470
or a specific group so that
people can say, oh, yeah.

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:20.330
That's the API
product I want to use,

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:23.560
because that is the one closest
tied to the application I

00:30:23.560 --> 00:30:24.630
want to build.

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:27.630
So let's say you want to
start getting started.

00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:30.865
Let's say that, going all the
way back to one of the earlier

00:30:30.865 --> 00:30:32.990
slides, that you are getting
a lot of this feedback

00:30:32.990 --> 00:30:36.500
that your API program has
APIs that are hard to use.

00:30:36.500 --> 00:30:39.260
Or just people are just having
a hard time understanding

00:30:39.260 --> 00:30:43.220
how your APIs work, or it's a
very high-touch kind of model

00:30:43.220 --> 00:30:46.040
where you need to constantly
work with their app developers

00:30:46.040 --> 00:30:50.060
just to get them onboarded have
them start consuming your APIs

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:51.650
and understanding your APIs.

00:30:51.650 --> 00:30:55.580
These are the common questions
that we ask our customers,

00:30:55.580 --> 00:30:58.700
and you probably want to start
asking yourself, and really

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:01.670
taking a hard look at your API
program and really saying, OK,

00:31:01.670 --> 00:31:05.600
how do we start down this
path of getting to that model

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:06.650
that we want to get to?

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:08.900
This whole outside-in model.

00:31:08.900 --> 00:31:11.703
One is identifying, who
are my API consumers

00:31:11.703 --> 00:31:13.370
and what applications
are they building?

00:31:13.370 --> 00:31:15.890
Once again, this is really
having a good understanding

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:18.170
of who's actually
consuming your APIs.

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:19.580
It's definitely the first start.

00:31:19.580 --> 00:31:24.210
If you have no idea who's using
your APIs, that is a problem.

00:31:24.210 --> 00:31:26.240
And so you want to
start addressing, really

00:31:26.240 --> 00:31:28.370
understanding who's
consuming your APIs, how

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:31.700
they're consuming it,
and what kind of feedback

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:33.860
that they have.

00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:37.207
The other one is, how
self-explanatory are the APIs?

00:31:37.207 --> 00:31:38.540
How easy are they to understand?

00:31:38.540 --> 00:31:40.743
We talked about RESTful
earlier on as well.

00:31:40.743 --> 00:31:42.160
When it comes to
payloads, when it

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:46.110
comes to API naming conventions,
is it straightforward enough?

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:48.820
If I had no idea what
the backend systems did,

00:31:48.820 --> 00:31:51.080
would I still be able
to piece together

00:31:51.080 --> 00:31:53.950
what an API could do based
specifically just on the name

00:31:53.950 --> 00:31:54.520
alone?

00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:56.530
Things like that.

00:31:56.530 --> 00:31:59.020
Do I have a developer portal
ready to support my community?

00:31:59.020 --> 00:32:01.240
Once again, developer
portal is just

00:32:01.240 --> 00:32:04.690
the tool, a mechanism to
interact with your application

00:32:04.690 --> 00:32:06.130
developers.

00:32:06.130 --> 00:32:09.290
So we highly recommend you
utilize something like that.

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:12.250
We've seen a lot of success
in having a developer

00:32:12.250 --> 00:32:15.880
portal be that mechanism, be
that tool for your developers.

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:18.740
What does your developer
onboarding process look like?

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:20.440
Once again, make it simple.

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:24.650
Make it automated, if it
makes sense for your business.

00:32:24.650 --> 00:32:27.772
The most common example we see
here for a developer onboarding

00:32:27.772 --> 00:32:29.980
process, this is very much
like how when you register

00:32:29.980 --> 00:32:33.760
for like an online
store, providing you

00:32:33.760 --> 00:32:36.850
with your email, maybe
some basic information.

00:32:36.850 --> 00:32:40.120
I will shoot you an
automated email confirmation,

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:42.440
just to confirm it's
a real email address.

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:44.020
And once that's done, bam.

00:32:44.020 --> 00:32:47.123
The registration
process is done and you

00:32:47.123 --> 00:32:49.540
can log in and start reading
documentation and potentially

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:52.390
even making some API calls.

00:32:52.390 --> 00:32:54.130
If you can get to
that kind of model,

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:56.990
that's a really good
model to get to,

00:32:56.990 --> 00:32:58.990
just because it's very
easy and it's very quick.

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:01.180
Within a matter of
seconds, really, I

00:33:01.180 --> 00:33:04.910
can start reading up
on API documentation

00:33:04.910 --> 00:33:07.540
and getting access to start
playing around with your APIs.

00:33:07.540 --> 00:33:09.760
That ties a little bit
into the fifth point

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:11.620
here, which is how
quickly can developers

00:33:11.620 --> 00:33:14.170
obtain an API key, as well.

00:33:14.170 --> 00:33:15.670
Where will developers
get answers

00:33:15.670 --> 00:33:16.850
when they encounter issues?

00:33:16.850 --> 00:33:20.080
So this is the whole ongoing
support and maintenance

00:33:20.080 --> 00:33:21.980
that we talked about earlier.

00:33:21.980 --> 00:33:24.700
And then finally,
what KPIs will I

00:33:24.700 --> 00:33:29.780
track to determine what
success looks like?

00:33:29.780 --> 00:33:32.260
So this is more of a kind
of an internal thing.

00:33:32.260 --> 00:33:34.722
But just to touch a
little bit about KPIs.

00:33:34.722 --> 00:33:37.180
And once again, there's a lot
of good documentation on KPIs

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:39.260
that are out there on
our Apigee website,

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:41.000
so I encourage
you to look there.

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:42.490
But when we talk
about KPIs here,

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:44.890
we're talking about
things like the number

00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:47.260
of active developers.

00:33:47.260 --> 00:33:48.550
Not the number of developers.

00:33:48.550 --> 00:33:51.580
The number of active
developers who are consistently

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:53.830
using your APIs--

00:33:53.830 --> 00:33:56.140
the number of useful APIs.

00:33:56.140 --> 00:33:58.030
A lot of times, we
see our customers

00:33:58.030 --> 00:34:03.550
think if they have 900 APIs that
shows a successful API program.

00:34:03.550 --> 00:34:05.710
We disagree.

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:08.560
It's not about the number
of APIs, but it's about,

00:34:08.560 --> 00:34:10.900
do you have a core
set of APIs that

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:13.989
can pertain to a lot of
different API products

00:34:13.989 --> 00:34:15.670
and a lot of
different applications

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:20.590
that can be easily reused for
a lot of various business cases

00:34:20.590 --> 00:34:22.120
and use cases and
things like that?

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:26.080
That is more useful than just
the sheer number of APIs.

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:28.630
And then also, of course, the
speed of onboarding traffic

00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:29.830
growth, direct revenue.

00:34:29.830 --> 00:34:32.320
There's a lot of different
ways of measuring the KPIs,

00:34:32.320 --> 00:34:35.050
but that's one thing you
want to keep in mind is,

00:34:35.050 --> 00:34:37.480
what are the KPIs
for your API program?

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:41.620
And how does that tie back
into this whole concept

00:34:41.620 --> 00:34:43.730
of a user-centric approach?

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:45.550
So once again, these
are just questions

00:34:45.550 --> 00:34:49.389
to consider that you
want to ask yourself.

00:34:49.389 --> 00:34:52.310
There are a lot of different
ways that you can approach it.

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:55.239
But as long as you can
approach it, once again,

00:34:55.239 --> 00:34:58.015
from this outside-in model,
that is something that we--

00:34:58.015 --> 00:34:59.890
the purpose of this
webcast is really

00:34:59.890 --> 00:35:03.340
to stress that in particular.

00:35:03.340 --> 00:35:07.378
All right, so that is just the
high-level overview of what

00:35:07.378 --> 00:35:08.920
we wanted to talk
about when it comes

00:35:08.920 --> 00:35:12.280
to this whole kind of
user-centric application,

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:15.640
consumer-centric
approach to APIs.

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:17.560
Once again, I've
only touched upon it

00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:19.060
at a very, very high level.

00:35:19.060 --> 00:35:21.400
I hope what you've
taken away from this

00:35:21.400 --> 00:35:26.710
is how we think of an
outside-in type approach, what

00:35:26.710 --> 00:35:28.150
are some of the
key things that we

00:35:28.150 --> 00:35:29.780
think are very, very important.

00:35:29.780 --> 00:35:31.600
We've only already
touched a little bit

00:35:31.600 --> 00:35:35.890
at a very high level what our
other customers have done,

00:35:35.890 --> 00:35:38.650
but you can hopefully have
seen the common trends

00:35:38.650 --> 00:35:41.290
of at least the approach
that they took when it comes

00:35:41.290 --> 00:35:42.970
to things like
RESTful API design,

00:35:42.970 --> 00:35:45.430
when it comes to things like
utilizing developer portal,

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:49.510
and just even in terms of the
mentality of how they switch

00:35:49.510 --> 00:35:52.180
when it comes to
API design, shifting

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:58.790
the mentality from an inside-out
model to an outside-in model.

00:35:58.790 --> 00:36:02.150
So with that, I can see that we
already have a couple questions

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:02.650
already.

00:36:02.650 --> 00:36:05.780
So I'm going to do my best
to get through all of them.

00:36:05.780 --> 00:36:09.400
If I don't get a chance to
get through all the questions,

00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:12.040
I definitely will answer
them either offline

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:14.992
either after the session is
done or within the chat itself.

00:36:14.992 --> 00:36:16.450
So if I don't get
to your question,

00:36:16.450 --> 00:36:17.780
I do apologize in advance.

00:36:17.780 --> 00:36:22.630
But I will get to as
many of these as I can.

00:36:22.630 --> 00:36:26.470
First question that
I can see here.

00:36:26.470 --> 00:36:28.720
"Can you elaborate on
the RESTful approach

00:36:28.720 --> 00:36:31.260
and how it ties to the
whole outside-in model?"

00:36:31.260 --> 00:36:31.760
Yeah.

00:36:31.760 --> 00:36:36.055
So RESTful approach,
kind of what

00:36:36.055 --> 00:36:39.350
I was talking about in
the middle of this webcast

00:36:39.350 --> 00:36:44.050
is this whole concept of, how
self-explanatory are the APIs?

00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:46.520
So those of who
are not familiar,

00:36:46.520 --> 00:36:48.250
the whole concept of
a RESTful approach

00:36:48.250 --> 00:36:51.850
is really things like
they have a certain way

00:36:51.850 --> 00:36:54.710
of doing like naming conventions
and things like that.

00:36:54.710 --> 00:36:59.050
And really, that's what
we wanted to emphasize.

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:00.020
From an API--

00:37:00.020 --> 00:37:01.960
I'll start with
naming convention.

00:37:01.960 --> 00:37:06.670
Are the APIs named in such a
way where I can very easily

00:37:06.670 --> 00:37:09.050
understand what the API can do?

00:37:09.050 --> 00:37:11.800
So this whole concept of
like the whole post and put

00:37:11.800 --> 00:37:14.980
and delete and the
verbs and all that.

00:37:14.980 --> 00:37:16.990
Really, the whole
RESTful approach

00:37:16.990 --> 00:37:19.120
is really to get
to that model where

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:22.360
there is a kind of a universal
common understanding of what

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:25.010
an API can do, just
based off the name alone.

00:37:25.010 --> 00:37:28.630
So that's what I mean by
ease of use for the API,

00:37:28.630 --> 00:37:32.220
following a RESTful approach.

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:33.390
All right, other question.

00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:36.240
"What do you mean when you
talk about API products?"

00:37:36.240 --> 00:37:39.870
Yeah, so just to reiterate,
when I talk about API products,

00:37:39.870 --> 00:37:43.320
once again, this is about
the bundling of the APIs

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:46.650
using that whole LEGO analogy
that I talked about earlier.

00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:49.260
API products-- and I
mentioned this before.

00:37:49.260 --> 00:37:53.970
It is really a core part of how
Apigee as a platform is built.

00:37:53.970 --> 00:37:58.320
We lean heavily into this
whole concept of API products,

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:01.350
because the way that we
see it is that if you can--

00:38:01.350 --> 00:38:04.350
there's one thing to
have an API catalog.

00:38:04.350 --> 00:38:06.930
But API products is
just another mechanism

00:38:06.930 --> 00:38:09.030
of-- if you take
a productization

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:12.540
approach or a way of
thinking of your APIs,

00:38:12.540 --> 00:38:14.130
then really, you're
starting to think

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:17.460
about packaging those
APIs to maybe one day

00:38:17.460 --> 00:38:19.500
get even to a
monetization type model

00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:24.030
where you're charging your
consumers per API call.

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:25.680
And really, the
concept behind that

00:38:25.680 --> 00:38:27.660
is, you want to start
thinking of APIs

00:38:27.660 --> 00:38:31.080
as, I have a lot of useful data.

00:38:31.080 --> 00:38:35.190
There are people internal,
external within our company

00:38:35.190 --> 00:38:37.410
that wants to have
access to that data

00:38:37.410 --> 00:38:39.870
because they can use that
data in a lot of cool ways

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:41.440
with their applications.

00:38:41.440 --> 00:38:44.550
How do I expose that
data out in APIs?

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:47.010
And if you can think about
it from a product model,

00:38:47.010 --> 00:38:49.530
like as if you're trying
to sell those APIs

00:38:49.530 --> 00:38:52.212
and there's some sort
of monetary return

00:38:52.212 --> 00:38:53.670
you're going to
get for those APIs,

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:56.190
that really is putting
you in the right mindset

00:38:56.190 --> 00:39:00.510
of how you want to think
about when you're exposing how

00:39:00.510 --> 00:39:01.260
your APIs.

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:03.360
If you're thinking from
that perspective of I

00:39:03.360 --> 00:39:05.760
have useful data, but how do
I roll it out in such a way

00:39:05.760 --> 00:39:08.950
where people will want
to pay money to use that,

00:39:08.950 --> 00:39:13.410
then that's definitely the
first step into a model

00:39:13.410 --> 00:39:16.020
that you want to get into
that leads to a successful API

00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:17.370
program.

00:39:17.370 --> 00:39:20.890
So long story short, that's
what we talk about API products.

00:39:20.890 --> 00:39:24.180
Once again, how it correlates
as you are thinking in terms of,

00:39:24.180 --> 00:39:26.460
as an app developer,
I really want

00:39:26.460 --> 00:39:29.970
to get access to that cool data
that you have in your back end.

00:39:29.970 --> 00:39:33.390
How would I want to make those
calls, as an app developer,

00:39:33.390 --> 00:39:37.190
putting yourself in their shoes.

00:39:37.190 --> 00:39:38.300
Next question.

00:39:38.300 --> 00:39:39.940
"When it comes to
the developer portal,

00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:43.970
should I start off big or can
it be iterated over time?"

00:39:43.970 --> 00:39:45.130
Great question.

00:39:45.130 --> 00:39:47.930
Personally, what I've seen
is the iteration approach,

00:39:47.930 --> 00:39:50.770
and that is the approach
that I would recommend.

00:39:50.770 --> 00:39:53.500
You're not going to get your
developer portal perfect.

00:39:53.500 --> 00:39:56.337
And this goes the same with
APIs themselves as well.

00:39:56.337 --> 00:39:58.420
You're not going to get
it right on the first try.

00:39:58.420 --> 00:40:00.670
There's going to be feedback
that you're going to get.

00:40:00.670 --> 00:40:03.550
And what you want to avoid
probably most importantly

00:40:03.550 --> 00:40:06.880
is, you don't want to
spend so much time trying

00:40:06.880 --> 00:40:08.920
to create the perfect
developer portal

00:40:08.920 --> 00:40:11.050
with the perfect
registration process

00:40:11.050 --> 00:40:15.700
that you elongate
your time to market.

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:18.350
You want to roll something
out there very quick.

00:40:18.350 --> 00:40:20.710
It may not have all the bells
and whistles, for example,

00:40:20.710 --> 00:40:21.627
like developer portal.

00:40:21.627 --> 00:40:25.060
It may not have a blog
or a forum capability

00:40:25.060 --> 00:40:27.610
to get right from
the start, but you

00:40:27.610 --> 00:40:29.638
want to roll it
out with your APIs,

00:40:29.638 --> 00:40:31.180
and you want to roll
them out quickly

00:40:31.180 --> 00:40:33.347
because you want to start
gathering that feedback as

00:40:33.347 --> 00:40:34.880
quickly as possible.

00:40:34.880 --> 00:40:38.815
So in this case, I definitely
would recommend that,

00:40:38.815 --> 00:40:40.600
in my opinion, as
long as you can

00:40:40.600 --> 00:40:45.280
get very easy-to-use kind
of registration process,

00:40:45.280 --> 00:40:48.760
maybe some look and feel tweaks
and some good documentation up

00:40:48.760 --> 00:40:51.130
and running, start
with the basics.

00:40:51.130 --> 00:40:54.100
Start from there and
iterate over time

00:40:54.100 --> 00:40:57.400
is definitely the
way that I would go.

00:40:57.400 --> 00:40:59.800
One thing that ideally
you would want to avoid

00:40:59.800 --> 00:41:02.680
is rolling out the APIs
in some sort of mechanism,

00:41:02.680 --> 00:41:07.407
god forbid it being some sort
of like 300-page API document

00:41:07.407 --> 00:41:09.490
or something like that
that you have to email over

00:41:09.490 --> 00:41:13.385
to all your app developers.

00:41:13.385 --> 00:41:14.760
The more quickly
you can roll out

00:41:14.760 --> 00:41:18.220
a developer portal,
the better, in my mind.

00:41:18.220 --> 00:41:21.100
But that was a great question.

00:41:21.100 --> 00:41:22.210
All right, let's see.

00:41:22.210 --> 00:41:24.940
"You mentioned capturing
feedback from consumers.

00:41:24.940 --> 00:41:27.250
What are some examples
of how that can be done?"

00:41:27.250 --> 00:41:29.020
Once again, another
great question.

00:41:29.020 --> 00:41:31.150
I'm going to go back to
the developer portal here.

00:41:31.150 --> 00:41:33.940
A lot of times when
it comes to feedback,

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:35.500
it can be done through forms.

00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:39.160
If you have this idea
of implementing a form,

00:41:39.160 --> 00:41:40.930
that could be one mechanism.

00:41:40.930 --> 00:41:44.530
On the developer portal itself,
there could be a feedback link.

00:41:44.530 --> 00:41:47.140
We talked about the role
of the API evangelist

00:41:47.140 --> 00:41:50.050
or maybe a product owner
or someone who can maybe

00:41:50.050 --> 00:41:52.930
proactively reach out to
application developers

00:41:52.930 --> 00:41:54.850
and get feedback there.

00:41:54.850 --> 00:41:56.920
The importance
here is not only do

00:41:56.920 --> 00:41:58.810
you want to capture
feedback, and there's

00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:01.430
a lot of different ways, as I
just described, of doing that,

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:04.240
but you want to make sure that
there is a process that you

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:08.260
build into your API program
and your software delivery

00:42:08.260 --> 00:42:10.990
lifecycle of, how do
we take that feedback

00:42:10.990 --> 00:42:13.845
and incorporate it into the
next iteration of the API?

00:42:13.845 --> 00:42:15.200
I think that's the trick here.

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:17.290
I think there's a lot of
times when people go down

00:42:17.290 --> 00:42:20.690
this approach, they think about
how to capture the feedback,

00:42:20.690 --> 00:42:24.100
but they don't really think
about how to act upon it.

00:42:24.100 --> 00:42:26.710
So when you're starting
to think about things

00:42:26.710 --> 00:42:30.880
like-- for example, we promote
an Agile type methodology when

00:42:30.880 --> 00:42:33.280
it comes to API development.

00:42:33.280 --> 00:42:36.130
The reason we promote that
is because with something

00:42:36.130 --> 00:42:38.770
like Agile or an iterative
approach to development,

00:42:38.770 --> 00:42:40.750
you can very quickly
turn on a dime

00:42:40.750 --> 00:42:44.980
and implement new feature
requests or new feedback you're

00:42:44.980 --> 00:42:46.990
getting much more
quickly than if you

00:42:46.990 --> 00:42:50.200
were to do something like in a
waterfall-type delivery model.

00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:51.850
So it's sort of all
related to that.

00:42:51.850 --> 00:42:53.475
You just want to make
sure that there's

00:42:53.475 --> 00:42:56.500
a good mechanism of getting that
feedback back to your product

00:42:56.500 --> 00:42:58.510
owners and your API
developers, just so

00:42:58.510 --> 00:43:00.460
that they can act upon it.

00:43:00.460 --> 00:43:04.112
That's another great
question as well.

00:43:04.112 --> 00:43:05.820
So given the amount
of time we have left,

00:43:05.820 --> 00:43:08.460
this is probably the last
question I'll answer live,

00:43:08.460 --> 00:43:11.100
and then we will maybe jump
into answering the rest

00:43:11.100 --> 00:43:12.210
of the questions via chat.

00:43:12.210 --> 00:43:12.780
Let's see.

00:43:12.780 --> 00:43:15.270
"Is the developer portal
for external users only?"

00:43:15.270 --> 00:43:17.340
OK, that's a great question.

00:43:17.340 --> 00:43:21.333
In my mind, and this is what
I tell my customers as well,

00:43:21.333 --> 00:43:23.250
your developer portal
should not differentiate

00:43:23.250 --> 00:43:25.170
between internal and external.

00:43:25.170 --> 00:43:26.940
And what I mean by that is--

00:43:26.940 --> 00:43:29.340
and this kind of goes
into the whole philosophy

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:30.900
of your API design as well.

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:33.330
You don't want to
design an API for--

00:43:33.330 --> 00:43:35.850
oh, these APIs are
for external consumers

00:43:35.850 --> 00:43:39.275
and I'm going to design my
internal APIs differently.

00:43:39.275 --> 00:43:40.650
From the end of
the day, you want

00:43:40.650 --> 00:43:44.580
to look at your API
consumers as one entity.

00:43:44.580 --> 00:43:46.530
Because at the end
of the day, everyone,

00:43:46.530 --> 00:43:49.470
whether they be internal
or external application

00:43:49.470 --> 00:43:51.750
developers within
your organization,

00:43:51.750 --> 00:43:54.250
wants APIs that are easy to use.

00:43:54.250 --> 00:43:56.940
They want to get access to the
APIs in a quick and efficient

00:43:56.940 --> 00:43:57.690
manner.

00:43:57.690 --> 00:44:00.210
So you should always
approach it and always

00:44:00.210 --> 00:44:04.770
try to strive to make your APIs
as easy to consume as possible,

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:08.460
regardless of if they are an
external partner or a person

00:44:08.460 --> 00:44:11.062
working at building an
application in their garage,

00:44:11.062 --> 00:44:13.020
or the person just sitting
on the opposite side

00:44:13.020 --> 00:44:14.730
of the building.

00:44:14.730 --> 00:44:16.990
You have much more
flexibility, of course,

00:44:16.990 --> 00:44:18.480
if there are internal
applications,

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:22.530
because maybe those
application developers already

00:44:22.530 --> 00:44:26.550
understand the backend services
and what that pertains.

00:44:26.550 --> 00:44:29.190
But you don't want to go
down the path of building

00:44:29.190 --> 00:44:32.220
kind of two different
versions of the same API, one

00:44:32.220 --> 00:44:34.560
for internal, one
for external, if you

00:44:34.560 --> 00:44:38.655
have the option of building one
API that both parties can use.

00:44:38.655 --> 00:44:41.110
From a maintainability,
it's a lot easier.

00:44:41.110 --> 00:44:44.790
But also just in terms
of happy app developers.

00:44:44.790 --> 00:44:47.370
Once again, putting
yourself in the shoes of,

00:44:47.370 --> 00:44:50.010
what can I do to make
their lives a lot easier so

00:44:50.010 --> 00:44:52.410
that they can go build their
applications much quicker.

00:44:52.410 --> 00:44:54.113
That is the approach
you want to take.

00:44:54.113 --> 00:44:56.280
So developer portal falls
in that same line as well,

00:44:56.280 --> 00:44:58.170
to directly answer
your question.

00:44:58.170 --> 00:45:00.540
When you're building
out a developer portal,

00:45:00.540 --> 00:45:02.430
a lot of times, we
tell our customers

00:45:02.430 --> 00:45:04.840
that they should build
one developer portal.

00:45:04.840 --> 00:45:08.520
Now, what the person can
see in that developer portal

00:45:08.520 --> 00:45:11.592
in terms of what APIs
are available to them,

00:45:11.592 --> 00:45:13.800
that's where something like
role-based access control

00:45:13.800 --> 00:45:14.790
will come in.

00:45:14.790 --> 00:45:17.370
As an external person
using the developer portal,

00:45:17.370 --> 00:45:21.120
I may not see all the APIs that
an internal user would see,

00:45:21.120 --> 00:45:23.940
but it should still
be one portal.

00:45:23.940 --> 00:45:27.150
Ideally, you won't want
to get into a scenario

00:45:27.150 --> 00:45:30.420
where you have to maintain and
upkeep two separate developer

00:45:30.420 --> 00:45:36.230
portals, from a cost and
operations perspective.

00:45:36.230 --> 00:45:38.670
So with that, I think that
is a wrap for the webcast.

00:45:38.670 --> 00:45:42.590
So once again, thank you so
much for spending the time today

00:45:42.590 --> 00:45:44.870
with me to go over this topic.

00:45:44.870 --> 00:45:46.550
Once again, it's
something that we

00:45:46.550 --> 00:45:48.020
see a lot with our customers.

00:45:48.020 --> 00:45:51.790
It's the ongoing message that we
promote a lot with our customer

00:45:51.790 --> 00:45:52.290
base.

00:45:52.290 --> 00:45:54.750
So I really hope that
you found this useful.

00:45:54.750 --> 00:45:57.890
I hope that some of the
questions we brought up,

00:45:57.890 --> 00:45:59.990
some of the topics
that we talked about,

00:45:59.990 --> 00:46:02.300
will get you thinking
about where you're

00:46:02.300 --> 00:46:03.920
at with your API program.

00:46:03.920 --> 00:46:06.170
And more importantly,
how you want

00:46:06.170 --> 00:46:08.900
to get to that ideal
state of having

00:46:08.900 --> 00:46:12.740
this whole user-centric
approach to your APIs.

00:46:12.740 --> 00:46:15.620
And once again, if there
are any questions you have,

00:46:15.620 --> 00:46:17.660
definitely reach out.

00:46:17.660 --> 00:46:20.060
We have a very robust
kind of community

00:46:20.060 --> 00:46:21.493
to answer your questions.

00:46:21.493 --> 00:46:22.910
Once again, I
mentioned before, we

00:46:22.910 --> 00:46:27.740
have a very great and robust
documentation site for Apigee

00:46:27.740 --> 00:46:30.090
that not only goes through
all the Apigee capabilities,

00:46:30.090 --> 00:46:33.283
but also even concepts like
RESTful API design, versioning,

00:46:33.283 --> 00:46:34.700
and all of these
different things.

00:46:34.700 --> 00:46:36.930
Developer portal, for example.

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:39.470
So I highly recommend
you guys take a look at

00:46:39.470 --> 00:46:43.252
that if that interests you.

00:46:43.252 --> 00:46:44.585
So that's a wrap on the webcast.

00:46:44.585 --> 00:46:46.070
So once again, thanks again.

00:46:46.070 --> 00:46:47.690
And I hope you guys
have a great day.

